{"name":"Mojave Desert","hex":"#bea684","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Mojave Desert","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["An arid desert in southern California, north and east of Los Angeles and southeast of the Sierra Nevada, the driest desert in North America."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Mojave_Desert"}],"entries":[{"title":"Mojave Desert","blocks":[{"text":"The Mojave Desert is a desert in the rain shadow of the southern Sierra Nevada mountains and Transverse Ranges in the Southwestern United States. Named after the indigenous Mohave people, it is located primarily in southeastern California and southwestern Nevada, with small portions extending into Arizona and Utah."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1f/Death_Valley_-_Mesquite_Flat_Dunes_-_dune_showing_stoss%2C_brink_and_slip_surfaces.JPG/330px-Death_Valley_-_Mesquite_Flat_Dunes_-_dune_showing_stoss%2C_brink_and_slip_surfaces.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mojave_Desert","wikidata":"Q184355"},{"title":"Desert","blocks":[{"text":"A desert is a landscape where little precipitation occurs and, consequently, living conditions create unique biomes and ecosystems. The lack of vegetation exposes the unprotected surface of the ground to denudation. About one-third of the land surface of the Earth is arid or semi-arid. This includes much of the polar regions, where little precipitation occurs, and which are sometimes called polar deserts or \"cold deserts\". Deserts can be classified by the amount of precipitation that falls, by the temperature that prevails, by the causes of desertification or by their geographical location."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/34/Rub_al_Khali_002.JPG/330px-Rub_al_Khali_002.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Desert","wikidata":"Q8514"},{"title":"Desert sand (color)","blocks":[{"text":"Desert sand is a very light and very weakly saturated reddish yellow color which corresponds specifically to the coloration of sand. It may also be regarded as a deep tone of beige."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/45/Sossusvlei_Dune_Namib_Desert_Namibia_Luca_Galuzzi_2004.JPG/330px-Sossusvlei_Dune_Namib_Desert_Namibia_Luca_Galuzzi_2004.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Desert_sand_(color)","wikidata":"Q9303163"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/21/Joshua_Tree_NP_-_Joshua_Tree_2.jpg/500px-Joshua_Tree_NP_-_Joshua_Tree_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/21/Joshua_Tree_NP_-_Joshua_Tree_2.jpg/1280px-Joshua_Tree_NP_-_Joshua_Tree_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/21/Joshua_Tree_NP_-_Joshua_Tree_2.jpg/500px-Joshua_Tree_NP_-_Joshua_Tree_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The Joshua tree (Yucca brevifolia) is endemic to the Mojave Desert.[12]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AJoshua_Tree_NP_-_Joshua_Tree_2.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/94/Desert_Electric.jpg/500px-Desert_Electric.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/94/Desert_Electric.jpg/1280px-Desert_Electric.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/94/Desert_Electric.jpg/500px-Desert_Electric.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A powerful High Desert summer storm 